/** \page checkpointing Combined application-state checkpointing

\section checkpointing_interface One application-facing checkpointer

mfem::Checkpointer binds live application state and owns saved checkpoint
representations. Store(id) captures complete continuation state and Restore(id)
restores it, including dependent solver objects. Callers do not pass a byte
snapshot between separate adapter and storage objects. Implementations may save
typed objects directly or use serialization internally. Borrowed application
objects must outlive the checkpointer.

mfem::StateId denotes an ordered logical application position, not physical time.
mfem::CheckpointId identifies a saved record independently of its physical slot
or filename. GetInfo(id) returns both identities. FindAtOrBefore(target, info)
chooses the greatest saved state at or before the target, breaking ties by lowest
checkpoint ID; on absence it leaves info unchanged and returns false.

Capacity() returns an optional checkpoint-count limit. No value permits growing
the number of separate snapshots without a predetermined horizon, subject to
resources and identifier ranges. Replacing an ID does not increase Size(), and
no operation silently evicts live records. Erasing an absent ID is a no-op.

\section checkpointing_direct Direct schedule execution

mfem::CheckpointSchedule produces mfem::CheckpointCommand values but does not
own or execute application transitions. A caller can use an ordinary switch:

- Advance: validate the live source position, propagate, verify the destination.
- Store: verify the live position and checkpoint identity, then Store(id).
- Restore: verify the named saved position, then Restore(id).
- Erase: verify the saved position, then Erase(id) without changing live state.
- Finished: verify the terminal position and finish.

For storage actions from_step and to_step denote the same saved StateId. Restore
and Erase do not require the live state to match that position beforehand.
Advance and Finished carry no checkpoint identity. Finished commands repeat
after completion. Reset rewinds a schedule without resetting application state
or removing saved checkpoints.

mfem::StoreEverythingSchedule generates Store 0, Advance 0->1, Store 1, ...,
Store N, Finished at N, assigning checkpoint ID state+1. It requires N+1 logical
records but generates commands incrementally without a horizon-sized array.
An offline schedule's finite budget is explicit even with growing storage.
mfem::IntervalSchedule saves state 0 and every K-th state strictly before its
terminal state N, assigning checkpoint ID state+1 and advancing one state at a
time. It needs 1 + floor((N-1)/K) saved records. The horizon and interval must be
positive; an interval at least as large as the horizon saves only state 0.
The terminal state is left unsaved so restoration requires some replay.
mfem::StatePropagator is an optional interface for application transitions;
applications may instead call their normal propagation functions directly.

The checkpointing miniapps supply application-specific classes derived directly
from Checkpointer and own their saved records. The heterogeneous example stores
complete typed copies and demonstrates all five actions. The Euler and mesh
examples also dispatch schedules directly. Independent references and replay
compare complete continuation state. Neither a controller nor a moving window
is needed.

\section checkpointing_errors MFEM errors and ownership

Use MFEM_VERIFY for runtime conditions and MFEM_ABORT for unrecoverable failures.
Missing required records, exhausted capacity, invalid states, incompatible
formats, and I/O failures follow MFEM's configured error action. The checkpoint
path has no explicit C++ exception handling and no rollback contract. The
miniapp explicitly selects MFEM_ERROR_ABORT. Capture leaves live state unchanged;
restoration validates saved representations before applying them where possible.
Operations are synchronous, and instances are not thread-safe.

\section checkpointing_miniapps Application-specific storage examples

Forward Euler integrates scalar cubic growth. Backward Euler integrates two
stiff diagonal decay modes using a closed-form implicit solve. Their miniapp
EulerCheckpointer owns complete solution, step, time, timestep, and operator
parameter records and reinitializes the solver on restore. Both support
memory-block, file-block, memory-snapshots, and file-snapshots, selected through
--storage; memory-block is the default. Blocks preallocate steps+1 fixed 64-byte
records and recycle slots without increasing payload extent. Separate records
grow by default; --max-checkpoints supplies an optional count limit.

The mesh example refines a changing nonconforming quadrilateral mesh. Its local
MeshCheckpointer saves complete MFEM mesh text, the refinement cycle, and the
element-selection index. It supports memory-snapshots and file-snapshots only,
defaulting to memory-snapshots. mfem::IntervalSchedule omits the terminal state,
forcing replay after restoration. Independent runs compare mesh structure,
serialized coordinates, continuation metadata, and reconstructed H1 fields.
Memory records retain map nodes and string allocations for reuse after erase.

File modes use --checkpoint-path to create a new directory and refuse existing
paths. Without a path they own a temporary directory removed on success.
Examples close file stores and reopen them against fresh application objects
before restoring and replaying. Explicitly requested stores remain on disk.
Both file modes validate native metadata, compatibility, lengths, and checksums;
an open marker rejects unclean stores. Reopening requires the same byte order
and real_t precision. File blocks use a fixed-size payload file and manifest;
separate mesh/Euler files have a growing manifest. Close() is checked and
idempotent. No crash recovery or power-loss durability is promised.

\section checkpointing_stages Subsequent implementation stages

Stages 1 and 6 are source-complete; Stages 2-5 are skipped. The removed generic
helpers and separate adapter/storage APIs have not been reintroduced. All
storage code is miniapp-local; reusable schedules belong to the library.
The remaining work in checkpoint-implementation-plan.txt is the optional
controller/FIFO moving window and further integration and fatal-error coverage.
Normal-path example cases are registered for every storage mode and optional
snapshot count limits; none have been executed.

No compilation or execution was performed.
*/
